{% extends "_layouts/default.njk" %}

{% block content %}
  <div class="nhsuk-grid-row">
    <div class="nhsuk-grid-column-two-thirds">
      <h1 class="nhsuk-heading-xl">Understanding your child’s record</h1>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="how-we-calculate">How we calculate vaccination status</h2>
      <p>To calculate a child’s vaccination status:</p>

      <ol class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--number">
        <li>We analyse a child’s vaccination records from a variety of sources such as GP practices, school vaccination services, regional child health information services and parents (for example, if a child was vaccinated in another country).</li>
        <li>We combine these records and list them, from the 1st vaccination onwards, to form a vaccination history for that child.</li>
        <li>We compare the vaccination history against the
        <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/routine-childhood-immunisation-schedule/routine-childhood-immunisations-from-1-july-2025-babies-born-from-1-july-2024">UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) routine schedule</a>
        to check that:
          <ul class="nhsuk-list nhsuk-list--bullet">
            <li>they have had all the appropriate vaccines for their age</li>
            <li>the 1st dose was after the minimum starting age for that vaccine</li>
            <li>they had the vaccinations with the correct length of time between doses for that vaccine</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>If a vaccination dose appears to be missing (if there is no record of it, or the child was too young, or it was too close to another dose) we show this on the vaccination status.</p>

      <h2 class="nhsuk-heading-l" id="extra-doses">Extra doses</h2>
      <p>Some children may get an extra dose of MMR that is outside the
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/routine-childhood-immunisation-schedule/routine-childhood-immunisations-from-1-july-2025-babies-born-from-1-july-2024">UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) routine schedule</a>.</p>
      <p>If a child is vaccinated before their 1st birthday (for example, because of a local outbreak or for travel) it will contribute to their immunity but they will still need 2 more doses to be fully protected.</p>
      <p>Similarly, if a child has their 2nd dose before the age of 18 months, or less than 4 weeks after an earlier vaccination, it will contribute to their immunity for travel reasons or a local outbreak, but they will need a 3rd dose to be fully protected.</p>
      <p>In rare cases, some children who have had 2 doses may need extra doses for medical reasons, for example, if they’ve had a blood transfusion, a stem cell transplant or chemotherapy.</p>

      <a class="nhsuk-back-link" href="/parent-test/">
        Back to scenarios
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
